6.21.m:
occupying a supreme celestial vehicle, moving everywhere, granting all desires, / surrounded by the maidens of Rudra for him who is detached from the branches;

delightful with the sound of dancing, singing, mṛdaṅgas, and other musical instruments, / adorned with divine garments, garlands, unguents, and ornaments;

filled with jars of divine nectar, brimming with divine water, / resembling ten million suns, very cool like ten million moons;

and having granted the celestial vehicle, swift as thought and going everywhere— / when his curiosity for pleasure is exhausted even after he has enjoyed pleasures,

causing his very fierce power to descend, which is naturally exceedingly difficult to attain, / wishing to burn down the forest [of saṃsāra], having the splendid radiance of the fire of dissolution;

having bestowed the import of the great mantra, Parameśvara [confers the realization]: / 'I am complete,' consisting in the constant contemplation, 'I am Śambhu.'

And having granted samādhi and unhindered supernatural powers, / which take the form of the vibration of devotion to himself and the ability to perform the work of the sun and others;

at the end of the life of the Lotus-born [Brahmā], the guru of the world / bestows upon him supreme liberation, devoid of return.
